## a b s t r a c t
The removal of $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{C}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{r}}(\mathbf{\boldsymbol{V}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{I}})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ from wastewater is one of the methods to ensure water safety. However, it is still a difficult point to design efficient and selective adsorbent. In this work, Cr(VI) and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ were removed from water by a new metal–organic frameworks material (MOF-DFSA) with numerous adsorption sites. The max adsorption capacities of MOF-DFSA were $188.12~\mathrm{mg/g}$ for $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ after $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ and $349.09~\mathrm{mg/g}$ for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ within $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . MOF-DFSA showed good selectivity and reusability after four cycles. The adsorption of MOF-DFSA was an irreversible process with multi-site coordination, and an active site adsorbed $1.798\ \mathrm{Cr}$ (VI) and $0.395\ \mathrm{Pb}$ (II). Kinetic fitting showed that the adsorption was chemisorption and surface diffusion was the main limiting step. Thermodynamic showed that $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ adsorption was enhanced at higher temperatures by spontaneous processes while $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ was weakened. The chelation and electrostatic interaction of the hydroxyl and nitrogen-containing groups of MOF-DFSA with $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ is the predominant mechanism, while the reduction of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ also play an important role in adsorption. In conclusion, MOF-DFSA was a sorbent that can be used for the removal of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})$ .
