## 1. Introduction
With the deterioration of the water environment, heavy metal ions in water are important factors leading to human diseases, such as sluggish development, renal insufficiency, anaemia, etc.
[1,2,3,4]. Even low concentrations of $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ can cause irreversible harm to the human body [5]. According to the World Health Organization (WHO), the maximum tolerable concentrations of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ for humans are 0.054 and $0.05\mathrm{\mg/L},$ respectively [6,7]. In order to achieve sustainability, it is necessary to selectively remove detrimental metal ions from wastewater in an effective way.
Toxic metal ions can now be removed using a variety of techniques, such as chemical precipitation, adsorption, biological treatment, reverse osmosis, photocatalysis, etc. [8–14]. Adsorption is regarded as one of the most promising technologies among them because of its high removal rate and easy operation. The surface complexing capacity, ion exchange capacity and adsorption performance of the adsorbent are significantly affected by its morphology, physical and chemical stability, hydrophilicity and surface charge [15–19].
Zeolites, graphene oxide, activated carbon, metal oxides and bio-sorbents have been widely used for the removal of $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ and Pb(II) due to their considerable specific surface area and abundance of active sites [20–25]. In recent years, metal–organic framework (MOF) materials [26,27] have attracted much attention for their high adsorption capacity and excellent selectivity. The researchers have synthesized several types of metal–organic framework materials for $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ adsorption, including $\{[\mathsf{C d}_{1.5}(\mathsf{b t c})(\mathsf{b i b p}){\cdot}2\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}]{\cdot}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ [28], $\mathsf{N i C o}(\mathsf{B D C})@\mathsf{M n O}_{2}$ [29], nZVI/ ZIF-8 [30], $\mathrm{Co-Al-LDH@Fe_{2}O_{3}/3D P C N F}$ [31], Non-carbon adsorbent Nano alumina (NA) [32], etc. These MOFs significantly enhance the adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ . However, their synthesis procedures are overly complicated, it is necessary to further develop a relatively simple method for the synthesis of adsorbent.
In accordance with Pearson’s acid-base theory, heavy metal ions, which are the soft acid, readily chelate with soft base functional groups containing N, O and S atoms to create stable coordination compounds, which remove hazardous $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ ions [33,34]. Additionally, materials with hydroxyl $(\mathrm{-OH})$ functional groups have a strong affinity for oxygen anions and are frequently used to remove $\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathsf{V I})$ anions [35]. In addition to allowing for greater pore volumes and specific surface areas, metal–organic framework materials that are synthesized with organic substances rich in adsorption sites also improve the adsorption ability. The removal of ${\mathrm{Hg}}({\mathrm{II}})$ ions from aqueous solutions was achieved by covalent bonding of polyamine-co-2,6-diaminopyridine on chitosan [36]. Elham et al. successfully removed Pb(II) from water by hydroxytriazine-based dendrimer/kaolinite nanotubes [37]. Marthi et al. achieved effective adsorption and desorption of lithium ions with the help of hydroxyl groups on layered $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TiO}_{3}$ [38]. The adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ was achieved by R. Ahmad with the aid of chitosan-grafted polyaniline-ommt nanocomposites [39]. Naushad removed $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ by YVO4: ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ nanoparticles [40]. Poudel efficiently removed heavy metal ion by MIL-88-derived MnFe-LDH and $\mathsf{M n F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ carbon nanofibers [41]. These studies showed that functional groups rich in nitrogen and oxygen $\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ , $-{\mathsf{O H}})$ are effective in removing hazardous metals ions including Cr (VI) and Pb (II) from wastewater.
Therefore, this experiment designed a new MOFs materials (MOF-DFSA) for the adsorption of $\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ . A one-step hydrothermal solvent approach was used to create the MOFDFSA, and its surface shape and characteristics were assessed. The effects of solution pH, contact time, initial concentration, temperature and co-existing ions on the $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ adsorption kinetics and isotherms were investigated. The removal mechanism of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ was also derived with the aid of relevant techniques. Thus, it provides a valuable reference for the removal of Cr (VI) and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ from wastewater.
## 2. Experiments and methods
## 2.1. Reagents
2,6-Diaminopyridine $(>99\%$ , DAP) and 2-Hydroxy-5- formylbenzoic acid $(>99\%)$ were supplied by Aladdin Chemical Co. Ltd. Zirconium tetrachloride $(\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}},98\%)$ , potassium chromate $\mathrm{\cdotK_{2}C r O_{4}}$ , $98\%$ , and lead nitrate $\big(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ , $98\%$ were purchased from Shanghai Macklin Biochemical Technology Co., Ltd. N,NDimethylformamide (DMF, $99\%$ ) and anhydrous ethanol $(99\%)$ were obtained from Tianjin Zhiyuan Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. All reagents are of analytical grade and require no further purification. The pH of the experimental solutions was adjusted with NaOH, HCl and ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ solutions respectively. The industrial wastewater was simulated for the laboratory.
## 2.2. The preparation of MOF-DFSA
The MOFs was synthesized using a one-step hydrothermal modulation method. First, 2-hydroxy-5-formylbenzoic acid $(4\mathrm{mmol})$ and 2,6-diaminopyrimidine (2 mmol) were dissolved in $150~\mathrm{mL}$ of anhydrous ethanol in a molar ratio of (2:1) and transferred into a three-necked flask $250~\mathrm{mL}$ . The reaction was carried out at $60^{\circ}C$ for $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After centrifugated, washed with ethanol four times and dried, a brown solid (2,6-amidopyrimidine-2-hydroxy5-benzoic acid, DFSA, $2.3{\mathrm{~g}}^{\cdot}$ was obtained. Then, $_{2\textrm{g}}$ of DFSA and $2.5\mathrm{~g~}$ of $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ solid were dissolved in $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of DMF solution. $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of HCl $(1\mathrm{\mol/L})$ was added dropwise and transferred to a hydrothermal reactor. After $130~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , the product was washed three times with DMF and anhydrous ethanol to remove unreacted material. Finally, the product was dried under vacuum $(60~^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The resulting burgundy solid was named MOFDFSA $(3.3\ \mathrm{g})$ . Scheme 1 gives details of the preparation of the adsorbent.
## 2.3. Characterization
X-ray diffraction (XRD) was used to analyze the crystallinity of MOF-DFSA (D8 Advance, Bruker, Germany). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM-EDS) was used to probe the structural morphology and elemental content distribution of MOF-DFSA (Supra 55, Carl Zeiss, Germany). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was collected with Mg-Ka radiation using a PHI-5000C ESCA system (PerkinElmer). The adsorption and desorption properties were measured by inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectrometry (Leeman Prodigy7, USA).
## 2.4. Batch experiments
The adsorption experiments were carried out on the basis of single-factor effects. $10~\mathrm{mg}$ MOF-DFSA was placed in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of a certain concentration of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ solutions $(100~\mathrm{{mg/L})}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ , respectively. The effect of different $\mathsf{p H}$ values on the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ was investigated by adjusting the pH of the solutions with NaOH, $\mathsf{H N O}_{3}(\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II}))$ and HCl $(\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I}))$ and shaking the suspensions for $20\ \mathrm{h}$ . Kinetic effects were obtained by setting different contact times ranging from 10 to $300\ \mathrm{min}$ at optimum pH (4.0). For adsorption isotherms, different initial concentrations of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ ( $\mathrm{100{-}600\ m g/L)}$ were used to react $20\mathrm{~h~}$ at $\mathrm{pH}=4.0$ . The sorption capacity $\left(\mathbf{q_{t}},\mathbf{mg/g}\right)$ and removal rate $({\pmb R}\%)$ were calculated from Eq. (1) and Eq. (2) as following:
$$
q_{t}=(C_{0}-C_{e})\times V/W
$$
$$
R=(1-C_{e}/C_{0})\times100\%
$$
Where $\pmb{C_{0}}$ and $C_{e}\left(\mathrm{mg/L}\right)$ are the initial concentration and the equilibrium concentration for metal ions, respectively. V (L) is the volume of the solution and ${\pmb w}({\bf g})$ is the mass of the adsorbent.
Desorption studies were also used to look into the repeated regeneration of MOF- DFSA. In the desorption experiments, the adsorbed MOF-DFSA was washed with thiourea solution $(10\%)$ containing 0.01 M HCl after adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ [42]. $40\mathrm{mg}$ of MOF-DFSA was dispersed in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ or Pb(II) solution $(100\mathrm{mg/L},\mathsf{p H}4.0)$ and shaken at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $20\mathrm{h}$ . After centrifugation, the used MOF-DFSA was added to $40\ \mathrm{\mL}$ of thiourea solution for $20\mathrm{~h~}$ . After solid–liquid separation, the regenerated MOF-DFSA was then washed several times with deionized water until it was neutral. Finally, the regenerated MOF-DFSA was again dispersed into a new $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ or $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ solution and stirred for $20\mathrm{h}$ to investigate its adsorption capacity. The regeneration efficiency $(\%)$ was calculated based on the adsorbed/desorbed amount of metal ions [43]. The adsorption–desorption experiments were cycled four times.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of MOF-DFSA
The SEM and EDS results of MOF-DFSA were shown in Fig. 1a and 1b. The surface morphology of the adsorbent was flat and a regular polyhedral structure. According to the EDS results in Fig. 1b, the elements in MOF-DFSA are C $(56.44\%)$ , N $(6.91\%)$ , O $(23.8\%)$ and $Z\mathrm{r}\left(12.85\%\right)$ . The chemical composition of the prepared adsorbent was further determined using XPS (Fig. 1c). The peaks of MOF-DFSA were mainly attributed to C1s, N1s, O1s and $Z\mathrm{r}3\mathrm{p}$ . These results were consistent with the designed target elements and the adsorbent was successfully synthesized.
As shown in Fig. 1d, the MOF-DFSA surface functional group type was examined using FT-IR spectroscopy to further confirm that the adsorbent was successfully produced. The strong characteristic absorption peak at $3189\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was attributed to the overlap of external water and potential hydrogen bonds $(\mathrm{-OH})$ . In addition, the FT-IR spectrum confirms a regular band of metal-carboxylic acid bonds in MOFs. The band at $1620~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponds to the symmetric and asymmetric stretching of the carboxylic acid bound to zirconium [44]. The peak at $1482~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is a benzene ring aromatic $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ and $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{=}\mathsf{N}$ vibrational band. The absorption band at $1385~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is derived from the $c{-}0$ bond while the absorption band at $1258~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is derived from the stretching vibration of the $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ bond [45,46]. The bands at 1088, 839 and $610~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to the phenolic hydroxyl bending vibrational peaks, $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ vibrations and $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -(OC) asymmetric stretching, respectively [44]. Fig. 1e shows the XRD spectrum of the MOF-DFSA. The crystallinity of MOF-DFSA is poor and the peak at $2\theta=5.3^{\circ}$ also indicates that the structure of the adsorbent is partially defective [47,48].
The pore size distribution of the material has a large influence on the adsorption capacity [49]. Therefore, the specific surface area and pore size were experimentally investigated using a nitrogen adsorption isotherm (Fig. 1f). Meanwhile, BET tests (Table 1) showed that the specific surface area of MOF-DFSA was $216.6746~\mathrm{\m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ with an average pore size of $8.5235\ \mathrm{\nm}$ At $\mathsf{p}/\mathsf{p}\circ=1$ , the abrupt change in gas adsorption is due to the accumulation of gas in the mesopores formed by structural defects in the MOF. Therefore, according to the IUP-AC classification, the MOFDFSA isotherm fits a typical type II curve [50]. In Fig. ${1}\mathrm{g},$ three regions of weight loss can be detected in the TGA curves with increasing temperature: (I) physisorption and lattice water (25- $192^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ; (II) decomposition of organics (DFSA) $(192–612^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and (III) metal oxides (Zr-O, $49.45\%$ . These results indicate that MOFDFSA was successful synthesized and exhibited excellent stability.
## 3.2. The effect of $p H$ on adsorption and zeta potential analysis
The pH of the solution has an impact on the surface charge density and adsorption capacity of the adsorbent during the actual adsorption process. Therefore, the removal rates of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}$ (II) at different pH values were investigated in Fig. 2a. The maximum removal rates for $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ were $95.41\%$ and $99.87\%$ respectively. At low pH values $(<4)$ , the active sites on the adsorbent surface were robbed by $\mathrm{H}_{3}0^{+}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ . Electrostatic repulsion had a significant effect on the adsorption of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ . The addition of hydroxide ions to the solution helps to deprotonate the adsorbent and promotes the adsorption of Pb(II) by ion exchange. When the pH increases, some of the ionic $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ is converted to precipitated $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ , resulting in weaker adsorption of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$
Additionally, $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ has a negative impact on $\mathsf{P b}(\mathsf{O H})^{+}$ and $\mathsf{P b}$ $(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ in high $\mathsf{p H}$ solutions, which decreased the removal of $\mathsf{P b}$ (II) [51]. For $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , it was mainly present as $\mathsf{H C r O}_{4}^{-}$ (or $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}^{2-1}.$ in solutions with $\mathsf{p H}>1$ [6]. The adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ by MOF-DFSA gradually increased in the range of $\mathsf{p H}<4$ , due to the gradual protonation of the reactive groups on the adsorbent surface under acidic conditions and the abundant $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ started to react with chromate. The maximum adsorption of $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ by MOF-DFSA was achieved at $\mathsf{p H}=4$ When $\mathsf{p H}>4$ , the reactive groups on the adsorbent surface gradually change from positive to negative and thus the capturing ability of adsorbent to $\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathsf{V I})$ decreases.
The zeta potential values of MOF-DFSA were investigated under various $\mathsf{p H}$ in order to further examine the impact mechanism of solution pH on the adsorption of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ . Based on the fact that $\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathsf{V I})$ was present in hydrochloric acid media, while Pb(II) was stable in nitric acid media. Therefore, the zeta potential of MOF-DFSA in the presence of Cl- and ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ was tested separately, as shown in Fig. 2b. With increasing $\mathsf{p H}$ , the surface potential of MOF-DFSA in acidic conditions steadily declines, reaching a minimum at $\mathsf{p H}=6$ . In contrast, the surface potential exhibits a negative trend in hydrochloric acid media with a pH range of 4–6 and in nitric acid media with a pH range of 5–6, which is mostly caused by the hydroxyl functional groups on the surface of the active adsorbent [52]. The isoelectric points in nitric and hydrochloric acid media are 4.52 and 3.44 respectively.
For $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ , when $\mathsf{p H}<3.44$ , the nitrogen group on the adsorbent surface is protonated and electrostatic repulsion occurs, which may inhibit the adsorption of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ . When $\mathsf{p H}>3.44$ , electrostatic attraction between the adsorbent (negative potential) and the metal ions (positive potential) can promote adsorption capacity. The highest removal of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ by MOF-DFSA was achieved at negative surface potentials $\cdot\mathsf{p H}>3.44)$ , due to the strong electrostatic interaction between the adsorbent and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ . Similarly, the maximum removal of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ was achieved at a positive surface potential $\mathrm{(pH}<4.52\mathrm{)}$ . Therefore, an optimum pH of 4 was selected for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ in all the next experiments.
## 3.3. Impact of contact time and kinetic analysis
The analysis of the equilibrium time dependence of the adsorption process improves our understanding of the adsorption mechanism and effectiveness of $\mathsf{P b}$ (II) and $\mathrm{cr}$ (VI) by MOF-DSA. The experimental investigation of the adsorption equilibrium times for the two metals was done separately. The effect of contact time on the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ is shown in Fig. 3a and b. The adsorption rates and capacities for $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ are lower than those for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ ions at all concentration conditions. This indicates that the adsorption sites on the adsorbent are more inclined towards the binding of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ . The adsorption process for $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ ions (Fig. 3a) reached equilibrium within an initial $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ for $100~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ and within an initial $180{-}240~\mathrm{min}$ for $150~\mathrm{mg/L}$ and $200~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ . However, the reaction rate for Pb (II) ions was much faster in Fig. 3b. On all concentrations, Pb (II) adsorption reached equilibrium within an initial $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ .
The adsorption kinetics and diffusion mechanisms were explored using pseudo-first-order (PFO, Eq. S(1)), pseudo-secondorder (PSO, Eq. S(2)), Elovich equation (Eq. S(3)), Boyd film diffusion (BFD, Eq. S(4)) and intraparticle diffusion (ID, Eq. S(5)) models [53,54]. The kinetic parameters of different sorption models for $\mathsf{P b}$ (II) and $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ were derived using a non-linear fitting method. The results were shown in Fig. 3c-d and Table 2. The appropriate fitted model usually requires that $R^{2}$ is close to 1, the predictive max adsorption capacity $(\pmb q_{e})$ is close to the experimental value $(\pmb q_{e})$ and the $\scriptstyle{R-\chi^{2}}$ value is smaller. For all ions, the $R^{2}$ values for PSO model was closer to 1 and the predicted $\left(q_{e}\right)$ was closer to the experimental $(\pmb q_{e})$ in all adsorption processes compared with PFO, implying that the adsorption process was chemisorption. The Elovich kinetic model is also often used to explain chemisorption, but it responds to multiphase interactions and has a non-uniform activation energy [55]. Table 2 shows that both $R^{2}$ and $x^{2}$ of the Elovich model outperform theses of the pseudo-second-order and pseudo-first-order models, indicating that the Elovich model is better to explain the adsorption behavior of $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ on MOF-DFSA, which also proves that the adsorption system is controlled by chemisorption process.
In addition, the Boyd film diffusion and intraparticle diffusion models were introduced in the fitting process, and the limiting steps between MOF-DFSA and $\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathsf{V I})$ $/\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})$ were investigated in depth to further analyze the adsorption mechanism. Fig. 4a and b and Table 2 show that the Boyd linear fit intercept is non-zero, demonstrating that the chemisorption process for the removal of Cr (VI) and $\mathsf{P b}$ (II) is dominated by film diffusion. Meanwhile, the slope values $(\pmb{k})$ for the three stages ID (Table 3) followed the trend of $k_{I}>k_{I I}>k_{I I I},$ indicating that the removal of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ by MOF-DFSA decreased with increasing contact time and reached the equilibrium state. The three constant values of the ID model $(C_{I},C_{I I},$ $(C_{I I I})$ , the high $R^{2}$ for $\mathrm{{ID-}_{i}}$ and the lowest $\scriptstyle{R-\chi^{2}}$ for $\mathrm{ID-j}$ suggest that membrane diffusion is the main rate-limiting step for $\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ adsorption on MOF-DFSA at low concentrations, while internal diffusion predominates at higher concentrations. In summary, the adsorption process of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ by MOF-DFSA was influenced by complex factors.
## 3.4. The effects of ionic concentration and isothermal adsorption
The adsorption isotherm, which establishes the dependence between the adsorbent and the adsorbate, is the basis for discussing the adsorption mechanism. Therefore, the effect of the initial $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ or $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ concentration on the adsorption capacity at different temperatures is shown in Fig. 5. As can be seen from Fig. 5 and b, the adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ or Pb(II) increased with increasing of the concentration and finally reached the adsorption equilibrium. In the initial stage, sufficient active sites on the adsorbent surface is good for the uptake of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ or $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ . As the binding sites on the surface are gradually occupied, the available active sites reduced and were eventually occupied. The equilibrium adsorption capacities of $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ at 298, 308 and $318\mathrm{~K~}$ were
188.97, 213.67 and $278.43\mathrm{\mg/g}$ . The equilibrium adsorption capacities of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ at 298, 308 and $318\mathrm{~K~}$ were 349.09, 279.79, $226.95~\mathrm{mg/g}$ , indicating that MOF-DFSA was more advantageous for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ ions. This was attributed to the higher affinity of the active site on the adsorbent for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ .
However, many details remain in this process: such as the analysis of the mechanism and the characterization of the adsorption equilibrium, which need to be further explored with the help of different mathematical models. Six adsorption isotherms, including Langmuir (Eq. S(6)), Freundlich (Eq. S(7)), Redlich-Peterson (R-P, Eq. S(8)), Hill (Eq. S(9)), Temkin (Eq. S(10)) and DubininRadushkevich (D-R, Eq. S(11)) [56,57], were used to insight into the adsorption behavior of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ or $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ by non-linear fits in Fig. 6 and Table 4, respectively.
The sorption of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ by MOF-DFSA was judged to be in accordance with the Langmuir $\left(0.9388{\sim}0.9437\right)$ , D-R $(0.9667{\sim}0.9752)$ , R-P $0.9648{\sim}0.9655,$ and Hill $(0.9775{\sim}0.9854)$ models with the help of the correlation coefficient $(R^{2})$ . Also, the Langmuir $(0.9316{\sim}0.9546)$ , Temkin $(0.9207{\sim}0.9469)$ ), R-P $\left(0.9416{\sim}0.9508\right)$ 1 and Hill $(0.9572{\sim}0.9616)$ models were according with the $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ sorption. The Hill model was well describe the adsorption process of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ or $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ on MOF-DFSA based on the correlation coefficient (Cr(VI):0.9775 0.9854; Pb(II): $0.9572{\sim}0.9616)$ , the max sorption capacity(Cr(VI): 189.55 269.266 mg/g; $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II}){:353.320}{\sim}237.809\mathrm{m}$ $\mathbf{g}/\mathbf{g})$ and the lowest $x^{2}$ values (Cr(VI): $52.287{\sim}136.776$ ; $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ : $410.121{\sim}102.116;$ . This indicates that the $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ or $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ adsorption is multi-molecular and that one active site can adsorb $1.798\mathrm{-}1.944\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})$ or $0.249{-}0.504~\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})$ . There is a positive synergy between $\mathrm{Cr(VI)/Pb(II)}$ and the MOF-DFSA binding site $(\pmb{n}_{H}>1),$ .
Notably, indicators such as the free energy of the isotherm $\cdot\mathbf{E},$ Eq. S(12)), the separation factor $\cdot\mathbf{R}_{L},$ Eq. S(13)) and the isotherm index ${\pmb G},$ Eq. S(8)) are also of great theoretical interest for the analysis of the adsorption mechanism. The isothermal indices for $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ adsorption by MOF-DFSA at different temperatures were 1.16, 1.20 and 1.21 and for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ were 4.17, 6.23 and 1.34 respectively, indicating that the Hill isothermal model, not the Langmuir model, was used for both $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ adsorption by the adsorbent. The adsorption potentials $\pmb{{\cal E}}$ of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ at $298{\sim}318\mathrm{~K~}$ were 8.10, 8.29 and $8.53~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ in order, while those of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ were 17.51, 12.76 and $28.17~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ , respectively, which proved that the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ or $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ on MOF-DFSA was chemisorption (including chelation or electrostatic interaction) [58,59]. Meanwhile, it also demonstrates that the adsorbent has a stronger binding capacity for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ .
On the basis of the Langmuir model, $\pmb{R}_{L}$ values are usually used to determine whether adsorption is favorable or not: $\pmb{R_{L}}=0$ , irreversible; $0<\pmb{R}_{L}<1$ , favorable; $\pmb{R_{L}}=1$ , linear case, $\pmb{R_{L}}>1$ , unfavorable. The $\pmb{R}_{L}$ trend curves for the dimensionless separation coefficients of $\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ at different temperatures are shown in Fig. 7. The $\pmb{R}_{L}$ values for both metal ions are between 0 and 1 in all cases. Also, the $\pmb{R}_{L}$ values vary inversely with the increasing of ion concentration. This indicates that at higher ionic concentrations, the adsorption of $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ on MOF-DFSA is an irreversible and favorable process in both cases. Meanwhile, the $\pmb{R}_{L}$ values for the three cases were $318<298<308$ K (Cr(VI)) and $298<318<308~\mathrm{K}~(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})).$ indicating that the irreversibility of Cr (VI) adsorption at high temperatures was enhanced by the target functional groups whereas the irreversibility of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ occurred under low temperature conditions. In addition, the adsorption performance of MOF-DFSA for $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ has been compared with other reported adsorbents listed in Table 5. The results show that MOF-DFSA has a better overall performance for the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ in water.
## 3.5. Temperature and thermodynamic investigations
