As can be seen from Table 6, both DS and DH are positive during the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I}).$ , indicating that the action of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ on the adsorbent is a heat absorption reaction and that the stoichiometry of the adsorption increases with increasing temperature. In contrast, the thermodynamic parameters for the adsorption of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ are the opposite, implying that the adsorption of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ is an exothermic reaction and that an increase in temperature suppresses the chaotic nature of the system, leading to a decrease in adsorption. This is in good agreement with previous experimental studies. Furthermore, the negative Gibbs free energy change for both metals indicates that the adsorption of $\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ on
MOF-DFSA is spontaneous and feasible. In particular, the Gibbs free energy of Pb(II) was significantly lower than that of $\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathsf{V I})$ with increasing temperature, suggesting that $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ is more autochthonous than Cr(VI).
## 3.6. The effects of co-existing ions and reusability
It is crucial to adsorbents that can selectively recover particular metal ions from co-existing ions because actual wastewater frequently contains numerous metal ions. The selectivity of $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ or $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ adsorption by MOF-DFSA was investigated by simulating industrial wastewater with a variety of metal cations and the availability of the adsorbent was analyzed. The ability of MOF-DFSA to remove $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ from solutions rich in other metal ions confirms its superior adsorption selectivity (Fig. 9a). Furthermore, the selectivity and adsorption capacity of the adsorbent for $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ remained almost constant in different media systems (Cl- (9b) and $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ (9c)).
Pearson’s theory suggests that both $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathrm{Pb(VI)}$ are boundary acid metals capable of chelating hard and soft acids [65,66], while the nitrogen and oxygen groups on the surface of MOF-DFSA are soft acids with good affinity for $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ . At the same time, the lone electron pairs on the nitrogen and oxygen atoms can cooperate with $\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathrm{Pb(VI)}$ to form stable $\mathsf{N}/0$ metal complexes, which are favorable for the adsorption of the target metal ions [67]. To quantify the adsorption selectivity of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II}).$ , the selective adsorption constant $(\pmb{K}\pmb{s})$ and partition coefficient $(\mathbf{K_{D}})$ were used to evaluate the metal ion selectivity. $(\pmb{K}\pmb{s})$ and $\left(\mathbf{K_{D}}\right)$ are provided by Eqs. S16-17 [68,69]. The selective results are shown in Fig. 9 (a-c). MOF-DFSA has better adsorption capacity for $\mathsf{P b}$ (II) with the highest removal rate. Also, the adsorption performance of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ was significantly improved compared to $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ , $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ , Ni(II) and Fe(III). Table 7 lists the measured Ks and $\kappa_{\mathrm{{p}}}$ . $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ has the highest $\pmb{K}_{S}$ value $(52.377~\mathrm{mL/mg})$ , indicating that
MOF-DFSA is more accessible to $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ . In addition, the ${\bf K_{D}}$ of MOF-DFSA for $\mathrm{Cr(VI)/Fe(III),Cr(VI)/Ni(II),Cr(VI)/Zn(II)}$ and $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})/$ $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})$ were 450.515, 972.54, 457.239 and 67.702, respectively (Fig. 9d).At the same time, the ${\bf K_{D}}$ of MOF-DFSA for $\mathrm{Pb(II)/Fe(III)}$ , $\mathrm{Pb(II)/Ni(II),Pb(II)/Zn(II)}$ and $\mathsf{P b(I I)}/\mathsf{C u(I I)}$ were 485.654, 1048.397, 492.903 and 72.982, respectively (Fig. 9e). In brief, the results clearly show that MOF-DFSA has strong affinity and selectivity for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ . These results demonstrate that MOF-DFSA can adsorb $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ more readily in aqueous environments where multiple ions co-exist.
To verify the reusability of MOF-DFSA, four adsorption/desorption cycles were carried out (Fig. 10a). The adsorption capacity of MOF-DFSA was slightly reduced with each cycle, which may be due to incomplete exposure of the adsorption sites or loss of adsorbent during cycling, which is consistent with the results of previous studies. This calculated loss of adsorbent mass after each cycle is in the order of $0.37\%$ , $0.684\%$ $0.92\%$ and $1.44\%$ . Meanwhile, the XRD spectra of MOF-DFSA before and after the adsorption of Cr (VI) or $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ showed that the purity of the crystals hardly decreases after multiple washings with deionized water and the adsorbent also exhibited excellent structural stability (Fig. 10b). This indicates that MOF-DFSA is a new reusable adsorbent.
## 3.7. Adsorption mechanism
The adsorption kinetics and isotherm analysis showed that surface adsorption and intraparticle diffusion of the monomolecular layer played a major role in the removal of $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ . To understand the morphological changes before and after adsorption, the microstructure was observed using SEM-EDS. After adsorption, white granules appear, which may due to $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ (Fig. 11(a-b)). In addition, signal peaks for Cr (VI) and $\mathsf{P b}$ (II) appeared in the EDS elemental analysis (Fig. 11(c-d)), indicating that the adsorbent achieved effective adsorption of $\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ . FT-IR not only allows the structure of the compound to be deduced, but also reflects the type of functional group [70]. New peaks corresponding to the stretching vibrations of $\mathsf{P b-O}$ and CrO can be found at 626 and $630~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ after the adsorption of Cr (VI) and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ [71]. This may be due to the interaction of the $-0\mathsf{H}$ terminus or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ terminus in the MOFs with $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}$ (II) to form ${\tt Y}{\cdot}{\cdot}0{-}\mathrm{H}$ (Y: Cr or Pb) [28]. The absorption of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ shifted the $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{=}\mathsf{N}$ stretching vibration from $1640~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to 1626 and $1620~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , probably due to chelation between Cr (VI) and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ groups. In addition, significant shifts in the $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ and $-0\mathsf{H}$ groups occurred at 1380 and $1113~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , both indicating that the nitrogen and oxygen groups formed stable coordination compounds with both $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ .
XPS is a modern method for materials analysis and is often used to analyze the chemical shifts of functional groups in materials. Fig. 12a shows the total XPS spectra of undoped MOF-DFSA and MOF-DFSA after adsorption of Cr (VI) (MOF-DFSA-Cr). Characteristic peaks for the Cr was observed in the XPS spectra of MOF-DFSACr. The peaks of 586.85, 586.41, 577.77 and 576.45 eV (Fig. 12b) can be attributed to Cr $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ (sat), Cr $\mathsf{2}\mathsf{p}_{1}$ , Cr $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ and Cr $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ [72,73], while the Cr peak in pure $\mathsf{K}_{2}\mathsf{C r O}_{4}$ is at $579.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ [40], suggesting an interaction with MOF-DFSA. In addition, the presence of Cr $2{\tt p}_{3}{\mathrm{~}}_{/2}$ (sat) and Cr $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ also indicates that some of the Cr (VI) was reduced to $\operatorname{Cr}(\operatorname{III})$ during the adsorption process. This not only achieved the capture of $\mathrm{cr}$ (VI), but also effectively reduced the toxic and hazardous nature of chromium. The XPS spectra of C1s,
N1s and O1s were analyzed in turn before and after adsorption. Three peaks can be found for C 1s before and after adsorption: 284.02 eV $\scriptstyle\mathrm{'}\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}/\mathbf{C}-\mathbf{N}$ and benzene ring), 285.60 eV (CAO) and $287.68\ \mathrm{eV}$ $({\mathsf{C}}{=}{\mathsf{N}})$ (Fig. 12c). The binding energies corresponding to the three peaks are reduced to varying degrees, indicating that the soft acids on the corresponding functional groups interact with Cr (VI). The N 1s spectra of high-resolution MOF-DFSA (Fig. 12d) are at $398.89\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $399.70\ \mathrm{eV}$ , corresponding to the N atom in pyridineANAC and $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{=}\mathsf{N}$ [74]. In addition, when MOF-DFSA adsorbs Cr (VI), the $\mathsf{C-N}$ peak at 398.89 shifted to $399.06\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{=}\mathsf{N}$ bonds at 399.70 shifted to $401.04\mathrm{eV}$ . The peaks representing both the $-N-C$ and - $\therefore C=N$ bonds shifted to higher energy bands due to forming more stable chelates. The O 1s peak in the MOF-DFSA spectra (Fig. 12e) contains two binding energies: 529.19 and $530.74\ \mathrm{eV}$ attributed to the $_{0-C}$ and $_{0-\mathrm{H}}$ bonds [75], respectively. The spectra of MOF-DFSA-Cr show new O1s peaks at 531.27 eV, respectively. Fig. 12f shows that the binding energy of Zr did not change before and after adsorption, which indicates that Zr is not involved in the reaction during the adsorption of $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ and only acts as a framework metal node. These confirmed the adsorption of Cr (VI) on MOF-DFSA.
(f) splitting of the $Z\mathrm{r}3\mathrm{d}$ peak in the XPS spectrum.
As shown in Fig. 13a, the main elements of C, O, N and Zr were verified by the XPS spectra before and after the adsorption of $\mathsf{P b}$ (II). After adsorption the new peak was Pb4f. As shown in Fig. 13b, the peaks, belonging to Pb $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ and Pb $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ precipitate at 143.00 and $138.14\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively. Compared to the binding energies of pure $\mathsf{P b}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ $\cdot4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ : 144.5 eV, $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ : $139.6\ \mathrm{eV},$ [76], these peaks are shifted to the lower energy band, which is caused in the interaction between Pb (II) and MOF-DFSA. The functional groups on MOF-DFSA that may play a role in the adsorption process were further investigated by the XPS spectra of C1s, N1s and O1s before and after adsorption. The binding energy of the three peaks of C1s can be found shifted to 283.95, 285.45 and $287.72\ \mathrm{eV}$ after adsorption. Only the binding energy of the $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ bond increased, indicating that the soft acid on this functional group interacted with Pb (II) (Fig. 13c). The XPS spectrum of N1s in Fig. 13d also demonstrates that the $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{=}\mathsf{N}$ functional group chelates with Pb (II) (399.70 to $400.88\ \mathrm{~eV})$ . The O 1s peak in the MOF-DFSA spectra (Fig. 13e) contains two binding energies at
529.19 and $530.74\ \mathrm{eV}$ , attributed to the $_{0-C}$ and $0{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ bonds, respectively. The spectra of MOF-DFSA-Pb show new O1s peaks at $532.23\ \mathrm{eV}$ . The adsorption of Pb (II) by MOF-DFSA was confirmed. Fig. 13f shows that the binding energy of $Z\mathrm{r}$ did not change before and after adsorption, which indicates that Zr is not involved in the reaction during the adsorption of $\mathrm{Pb(VI)}$ and only acts as a framework metal node. Furthermore, MOF-DFSA contains only oxygen functional group: the hydroxyl group $(-\mathsf{O H})$ , which indicates that the hydroxyl group interacts with the adsorbate.
Due to the influence of electrostatic interactions and $\mathsf{p H}$ on the adsorption kinetics of MOF-DFSA, the adsorption ability of the adsorbent to adsorb metal ions increases with increasing pH (decreasing the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ concentration). In addition, FTIR spectra (Fig. 11e) and XPS photoelectron spectra (Fig. 12 and Fig. 13)
clearly show that the peak positions and energy spectra of MOFDFSA are displaced to different degrees after the adsorption of Cr (VI) and $\mathsf{P b}$ (II). The results indicate that the interaction of $\upmu$ -OHcontaining and nitrogen-containing functional groups with Cr (VI) and $\mathsf{P b}$ (II) is dominated by chelation. In addition, the reduction in the adsorption of Cr (VI) is also present. Furthermore, the mechanism of Cr (VI) and Pb (II) on MOF-DFSA is also coordination and electrostatic action in Scheme 2.
## 4. Conclusion
A new adsorbent for the selective removal of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ or $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ from water was developed in one-step by introducing hydroxyl/ amino active sites. Firstly, the successful synthesis of the adsorbent was verified by XPS, FE-SEM, EDS, XRD and FT-IR. The XRD results show that the prepared MOF-DFSA has an amorphous structure with large specific surface area. Furthermore, The maximum adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ by MOF-DFSA is $188.12~\mathrm{mg/g}$ at pH 4 after $120\ \mathrm{min}$ . The maximum adsorption capacity of Pb(II) by MOF-DFSA is $349.09~\mathrm{mg/g}$ at $\mathsf{p H}4$ after $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The adsorption processes for both metal ions fit well with the Hill isotherm and Elovich kinetics, suggesting it is a uniform monolayer adsorption. The chemisorption of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ or $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ on MOF-DFSA is dominated by multisite co-action and film surface diffusion, and one binding site of MOF-DFSA has the capacity to bind $1.798{\mathrm{~Cr}}({\mathrm{VI}})$ and 0.395 $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ . Further thermodynamic confirmed the adsorption process of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ is exothermic, while that of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ is endothermic. In addition, MOF-DFSA shows good selectivity and reusability for $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ , with all hydroxyl and nitrogen-containing groups contributing to enhance $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ accumulation; whereas for $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ only the hydroxyl and $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ groups are relevant. After four successive adsorption cycles, the removal efficiency of $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ ions was $89.42\%$ and $90.45\%$ respectively, and the crystal structure did not change. Finally, the mechanism is chelation, reduction and electrostatic interaction between $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ and MOF-DFSA; while the mechanism of $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ adsorption is chelation and electrostatic interaction. The results show that MOF-DFSA is a powerful sorbent that can be used for the removal of $\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathsf{V I})$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ .
